@using MUI.Web.Icons

@*
    A game's face: the icon it published, where we hold one, and its monogram otherwise.

    Served from this origin rather than the game's. Hot-linking would hand every reader's address,
    user-agent and referring URL to a third-party server for a decoration — on a listing, to five
    hundred of them at once — which is what §11 exists to prevent.

    A game with no icon gets no element saying so. There is no placeholder, no broken image and no
    "logo unavailable": that we could not reach somebody's web server is a fact about our afternoon
    and not about their game (rule 5), and the monogram is what a game with nothing declared gets
    anyway. The two states are drawn identically on purpose.

    aria-hidden on the monogram and alt="" on the image, both because the game's name is the link
    immediately beside this — a screen reader announcing the picture as well would read it twice.
*@

@if (HasIcon)
{
    <img class="plate icon" alt="" src="@IconEndpoint.For(Slug)"
         width="@Size" height="@Size" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
}
else if (Fallback)
{
    <div class="plate mono" aria-hidden="true">@Monogram.Of(Name)</div>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Slug { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Whether we hold bytes for this game — never whether it declared an icon.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HasIcon { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a game with no icon gets a monogram, or gets no element at all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Off on the game page, per the handoff.</b> "When absent, render no element at all. No
    /// monogram, no grey square, no initial in a circle." Two reasons, and both hold: an empty
    /// frame on five hundred pages is noise, and a generated monogram invents a brand the game never
    /// supplied — which on a site whose whole claim is that it publishes only what it measured is
    /// the one kind of decoration that costs something. The title simply starts at the left edge,
    /// and because the identity block is a flex row with a gap, the indent collapses on its own.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The parameter exists rather than the behaviour being unconditional because a surface that
    /// needs a fixed left edge down a list of rows is a different argument from a surface showing
    /// one game. Nothing passes it today.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public bool Fallback { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The box the plate occupies, which the stylesheet fixes and this repeats on the element.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The attributes are what reserve the space before the bytes arrive, and on a listing of five
    /// hundred lazily-loaded images that is the difference between a page that settles and one that
    /// reflows under the reader as they scroll. It is the box and not the image's own dimensions —
    /// <c>object-fit: contain</c> letterboxes the artwork inside it rather than cropping it, so a
    /// logo that is not square is still the shape its author drew.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public int Size { get; set; } = 36;
}
